finding: The stage has not decided the cause. The flag A/B strongly links the red to the WASM tiering regime but does not identify the 232/368-byte allocation. In the installed Node's V8 14.6.202 source, Runtime_WasmTriggerTierUp encloses the tier-up work in DisallowGarbageCollection; its interrupt handling can allocate separately. Feedback-vector allocation and JS-to-WASM wrapper compilation are separate heap-allocating paths. SamplingHeapProfiler uses JavaScriptStackFrameIterator, so any of these or a real boundary allocation can end at solve without a WASM callee frame. The commit supplies no native-frame or other allocation-identifying evidence to distinguish them, as stage 1 explicitly requires. An independent i64 export control measured 6960 bytes under callWasm in 600 calls both with and without the flag, so ordinary steady boundary allocation is still observable, but neither that nor the planted JS object identifies the intermittent bytes. Also the new comment and commit title overclaim: --no-wasm-dynamic-tiering requests eager background TurboFan tier-up, not completion at the top tier before sampling. V8 GetDefaultTiersPerModule still selects Liftoff as baseline with this flag and TurboFan as the separate top tier. Node's JS optimization-trace refusal does not establish WASM completion, and display.ts forwards the flag to Chromium without any such refusal. The comment's claim that WebAssembly compiles to its top tier up front is therefore not guaranteed.
